WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.042
Every API client should have an explicit trust model.

2
00:00:03.042 --> 00:00:13.116
Internal APIs, external APIs, partner APIs, cloud APIs, identity APIs,
webhook callbacks, service-to-service calls, gateways, and proxies all
move data across boundaries.

3
00:00:13.116 --> 00:00:19.945
The boundary may be between applications, business units, networks,
tenants, vendors, regions, or privilege levels.

4
00:00:19.945 --> 00:00:29.478
A secure design names those boundaries so the client can apply the right
controls at the point where data, identity, credentials, destination, or
assumptions change.

5
00:00:29.478 --> 00:00:33.602
Configured endpoints are different from user-supplied destinations.

6
00:00:33.602 --> 00:00:44.623
A client that always calls a known service from configuration has a
narrower risk profile than a client that accepts a URL, host, tenant
endpoint, webhook target, or callback address from input.

7
00:00:44.623 --> 00:00:47.463
Environment-specific endpoints also need care.

8
00:00:47.463 --> 00:00:57.604
Development, staging, production, and disaster recovery may use different
destinations, but each environment should still define what is allowed and
what data may flow there.

9
00:00:57.604 --> 00:01:01.458
Internal APIs should not be trusted only because they are internal.

10
00:01:01.458 --> 00:01:10.383
Internal services can be misconfigured, overexposed, compromised, called
from unexpected paths, or given broader access than their callers should
have.

11
00:01:10.383 --> 00:01:20.659
A service-to-service request still needs authentication, authorization,
request validation, response validation, and monitoring appropriate to the
impact of the operation.

12
00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:25.798
Network location can support a control model, but it should not be the
whole control model.

13
00:01:25.798 --> 00:01:29.314
Good architecture also defines ownership and failure modes.

14
00:01:29.314 --> 00:01:39.455
Someone should own the integration, the credentials, the data contract,
the allowed destinations, the retry policy, the logging expectations, and
the incident response path.

15
00:01:39.455 --> 00:01:49.462
The design should explain what happens when the remote service is slow,
unavailable, returns malformed data, changes a contract, denies access, or
produces partial results.

16
00:01:49.462 --> 00:01:54.600
Those choices affect security because they shape what the application does
under stress.
